Let an element opt out of touch-to-mouse emulation with `on_touch`, and deliver taps as `ClickEvent::Touch`.

What: `TouchEvent` becomes a `MouseEvent`, so `Window::on_mouse_event` can listen for raw touches. The window first
sends each started touch to those listeners. A listener that calls `prevent_default` claims the touch, the same claim
rule `TouchDragEvent` and `LongPressEvent` use. A claimed touch is routed to the listeners (`TouchRoute::Listeners`)
for every later phase, hit-tested at its start position. It never reaches the gesture recognizer, so no mouse press,
click, scroll or long press is synthesized from it. Unclaimed touches go to the recognizer as before
(`TouchRoute::Gestures`).

`Interactivity::on_touch` / `InteractiveElement::on_touch` is the element API: it registers a touch listener on the
element's hitbox and claims touches that start over it.

Separately, div's click handling now builds the declared `ClickEvent::Touch(TouchClickEvent)` when the press and
release were synthesized from a tap (per `Window::pointer_source()`, patch 0003), so `on_click` listeners can tell
taps from clicks.

Why: upstream synthesizes mouse events for every tap and gives elements no raw touches (the `TouchEvent` docs call
per-touch dispatch "pending"), and `ClickEvent::Touch` is declared but never built. Drawing surfaces, sliders and
custom gestures need raw touches without a stray click or scroll.

Depends on: 0003 (`Window::pointer_source`; `PointerDevice` in tests).

Test: `touch_listeners_opt_an_element_out_of_touch_to_mouse` (div.rs), with rows for tap, pan and mouse click, each with
and without `on_touch`.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

